        "A product page should answer the customer's questions in the order they arise. It should not rely on exaggerated claims, fake countdown timers, invented reviews, or confusing discounts.\n8.1 Recommended page structure\n1. Clear headline: identify the product and its main everyday use.\n2. Product visual: show the real item accurately.\n3. Primary benefit: explain the main reason a customer might want it.\n4. How it works: describe the mechanism or usage without exaggeration.\n5. Features and specifications: include dimensions, materials, compatibility, contents, and limitations.\n6. Demonstration: show a real or clearly labelled illustrative use case.\n7. What's included: make the package contents explicit.\n8. Price and offer: show the total price and any genuine promotion terms.\n9. Shipping information: give a realistic delivery estimate.\n10. Returns and support: make the policy easy to find.\n11. FAQs: answer the most likely objections.\n12. Call to action: use a clear, accurate purchase action.\n8.2 Trust checklist\n- [ ] The page accurately represents the product.\n- [ ] The price and currency are clear.\n- [ ] No fake reviews or fabricated testimonials appear.\n- [ ] No fake scarcity or false “only a few left” messages appear.\n- [ ] Shipping costs and delivery estimates are disclosed.\n- [ ] Return and refund terms are understandable.\n- [ ] Contact information works.\n- [ ] Checkout works on mobile and desktop.\n- [ ] The store does not imitate a competitor's brand.\n- [ ] Every performance or health claim is supportable.\n8.3 Offer design\nThe offer is more than a discount. It includes the product, price, bundle, shipping, guarantee or return terms, and the reason to buy now.",
