        "This is a useful research signal, but it is not proof of profitability. An ad may remain live for reasons unrelated to performance, and visible impressions do not reveal the advertiser's margins or actual sales.\n9.1 Research a competitor ad systematically\nFor each relevant ad, record:\n- Brand and product\n- Target audience implied by the creative\n- Opening hook\n- Problem or desire being addressed\n- Main promise\n- Demonstration or proof\n- Emotional tone\n- Objections addressed\n- Offer and call to action\n- Format, length, and pacing\n- Visible engagement or longevity signals\n- What you can learn without copying\n9.2 Read the ad, not just the video\nThe source's most useful marketing lesson is to ask:\n- What emotion is the ad trying to create?\n- What problem is it solving?\n- What does the opening hook do?\n- Why might the intended customer care?\n- What proof is offered?\n- What action does the ad ask the viewer to take?\nAn ad is a sequence of decisions. The product alone does not explain why the customer buys.\n9.3 Inspiration versus copying\nUse competitor ads to understand the market. Do not copy their scripts word for word, reproduce their footage, clone their branding, use their customer testimonials, or create a near-identical ad with a different face.",
