        "The source also describes a document for making internet personalities appear to hold the product. That technique raises consent, impersonation, and deception concerns. Do not take a real person's likeness or voice and make them appear to endorse a product without explicit permission. Do not fabricate a doctor, expert, customer, or testimonial. An AI avatar must not be presented as a real qualified professional or actual customer.\n10.1 A safer AI creative workflow\n1. Identify the audience and one specific customer problem.\n2. Choose one advertising angle.\n3. Write an original script using only supportable claims.\n4. Decide whether to use a real creator, a licensed avatar, or a clearly disclosed AI presenter.\n5. Use a product visual that accurately depicts the item.\n6. Create a short video with a clear opening, explanation, demonstration, and call to action.\n7. Add captions and check that they are accurate.\n8. Review every frame for misleading implications.\n9. Check music, footage, fonts, and avatar rights.\n10. Export multiple variations that test one meaningful difference at a time.\n10.2 Choose a presenter that fits the message\nThe source selects a doctor-like avatar because the chosen product is a health supplement. That choice can create an implied medical endorsement. Unless the presenter is a real, appropriately qualified professional who has genuinely reviewed the product and authorised the endorsement—and all claims comply with applicable rules—do not use a medical persona to sell a health product.",
        "For ordinary consumer products, select a presenter based on audience relevance, tone, and clarity. A presenter should not imply expertise they do not possess.\n10.3 A simple video structure\nSegment\tPurpose\tExample direction\n0–3 seconds\tHook\tStart with a relatable situation or question\n3–8 seconds\tProblem\tDescribe the customer's everyday frustration\n8–16 seconds\tProduct introduction\tShow the product and explain what it does\n16–25 seconds\tDemonstration\tShow the actual product in use\n25–32 seconds\tObjection\tAnswer one realistic concern\nFinal seconds\tCall to action\tExplain where to learn more or buy",
