      "id": "chapter-13",
      "number": 13,
      "title": "Chapter 13 — Stop, iterate, or scale",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The source's scaling lesson is to identify the strongest ad, create more variations of it, and allocate more budget when performance supports the decision. It also describes increasing budgets aggressively during the later day, then reducing the budget after the operator felt the scaling was too fast.",
        "The general principle is sound: scale what has evidence behind it, not what merely feels exciting. But aggressive budget changes can increase volatility and expose the business to losses.\n13.1 Three possible decisions\nStop\nStop or pause when:\n- The product has a serious quality or compliance issue.\n- The supplier cannot meet the promised fulfilment standard.\n- Checkout or tracking is broken.\n- The campaign reaches the pre-set loss limit.\n- The economics cannot support the observed CPA.\n- Customer feedback reveals a fundamental product problem.\nStopping is a valid business decision. Do not keep spending simply because you have already spent money.\nIterate\nIterate when there is a plausible signal but a specific bottleneck:\n- Strong clicks but weak add-to-cart rate: improve page clarity and offer.\n- Strong add-to-cart rate but weak purchases: inspect checkout and shipping.\n- One creative has better engagement: develop genuinely distinct versions of its angle.\n- Customers misunderstand the product: improve demonstrations and FAQs.\n- CPA is close to break-even: test a meaningful offer or creative improvement without increasing risk blindly.\nChange one major variable at a time where possible.\nScale\nConsider scaling only when:\n- Purchases are real and tracking is reliable.\n- The campaign has enough data to reduce the chance of acting on noise.\n- Contribution after ads is positive.\n- Supplier stock and fulfilment can handle additional orders.\n- Refunds, complaints, and chargebacks are acceptable.\n- Customer support can handle higher volume.\n- Cash flow can fund advertising and supplier payments before payouts arrive.\nIncrease spend gradually and review the effect. A winning ad can stop performing as audiences saturate, competition changes, or delivery costs rise.\n13.2 Why one winning ad matters\nThe source observes that one ad generated most of the purchases in the initial test. This is a useful reminder that ad performance is often uneven: a small number of creatives may account for much of the result.",
        "The response should be to understand why the ad worked:\n- Was the opening more relevant?\n- Was the demonstration clearer?\n- Did it address a stronger customer problem?\n- Did it show the product more convincingly?\n- Was the offer easier to understand?\n- Did the audience match the message?\nThen create original variations around the insight. Do not simply duplicate the same asset repeatedly."
      ]
