      "id": "chapter-14",
      "number": 14,
      "title": "Chapter 14 — The marketing lesson: sell the outcome, not just the item",
      "paragraphs": [
        "One of the source's strongest points is that product selection and marketing are different skills. A product does not sell only because it exists or is trending. Customers respond to the problem, emotion, identity, convenience, or desired outcome associated with it.",
        "The source describes competitor research as asking what emotion an ad targets, what problem it solves, and what the hook is doing. This is more useful than simply copying a product category.\n14.1 Translate features into customer value\nProduct feature\tCustomer-facing question\nCompact size\tWhere does it fit or travel?\nFaster setup\tWhat task becomes easier?\nReusable design\tWhat repeated inconvenience does it reduce?\nMultiple functions\tWhich separate items or steps might it replace?\nSimple operation\tWho benefits from a lower learning curve?",
        "Only use benefits that are accurate for the product.\n14.2 Build an angle map\nFor the selected product, write down:\n- Customer segment\n- Situation or trigger\n- Problem or desire\n- Current alternative\n- Product feature\n- Credible benefit\n- Evidence or demonstration\n- Likely objection\n- Appropriate call to action\nA single product may have several legitimate audiences or use cases. Test them separately rather than making one ad try to speak to everyone.\n14.3 Community and identity\nThe source argues that people sometimes buy the feeling associated with a brand or the sense of belonging around it. That can be a useful positioning insight, but do not manufacture fake communities or pretend to have a customer base that does not exist.",
        "Build trust through consistent product quality, helpful support, clear communication, and a brand voice that respects customers."
      ]
