      "title": "Chapter 15 — Risk, compliance, and customer protection",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A fast launch does not reduce legal or ethical responsibilities. The source's example of a health supplement makes this especially important.\n15.1 Product-category risk\nSome categories require additional checks, such as:\n- Supplements and foods\n- Cosmetics and skincare\n- Medical or health-related products\n- Children's products\n- Electronics and batteries\n- Products with safety certifications\n- Branded or copyrighted goods\n- Products subject to import restrictions\nCheck the laws and platform policies for the seller's location and the destination market. Do not assume that a product allowed on a supplier marketplace is automatically legal to advertise or sell in every country.\n15.2 Advertising claims\nDo not publish claims that are:\n- Unsupported by evidence\n- Guaranteed or absolute when results vary\n- Medical or disease-treatment claims without the necessary authorisation and substantiation\n- Based on fabricated statistics\n- Presented as expert advice by an unqualified or fictional professional\n- Misleading about typical customer outcomes\nAI-generated copy must be reviewed by a human before publication.\n15.3 AI identity and endorsements\n- Do not clone a real person's face or voice without permission.\n- Do not make a person appear to endorse a product when they have not.\n- Do not fabricate customer testimonials or before-and-after results.\n- Do not imply that an AI avatar is a real doctor, expert, or customer.\n- Disclose synthetic or materially altered media when required by platform rules or law.\n- Use properly licensed avatar, voice, music, and stock assets.\n15.4 Consumer transparency\nProvide accurate information about:\n- Seller identity and contact details\n- Product specifications\n- Total price and applicable charges\n- Delivery timelines\n- Returns, refunds, and cancellations\n- Warranty or guarantee terms\n- Data collection and privacy\n- Customer support\nFor an India-based seller, investigate applicable Indian consumer-protection, ecommerce, GST, import, payment, and data-protection requirements, as well as the rules of the destination country. Requirements depend on the business structure, product, transaction, and market. Obtain qualified advice where necessary.\n15.5 Cash-flow risk\nA store may need to pay the supplier and advertising platform before the payment processor releases customer funds. Refunds and chargebacks can arrive after the original sale. Maintain a reserve and do not treat dashboard revenue as cash available to spend."
      ]
