      "id": "chapter-18",
      "number": 18,
      "title": "Chapter 18 — A 30-day improvement plan",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A 24-hour sprint can produce a first test. A real business is built by improving the offer, creative, fulfilment, and customer experience over time.\nDays 1–3: Validate the foundation\n- Confirm the product and supplier.\n- Verify product claims and category requirements.\n- Order a sample if feasible.\n- Calculate unit economics.\n- Review the product page and policies.\n- Test checkout, payment, tracking, and confirmation emails.\n- Do not scale until the operational foundation is sound.\nDays 4–7: Run a controlled creative test\n- Launch within the pre-defined budget.\n- Test a small set of genuinely different creative angles.\n- Monitor funnel quality and tracking.\n- Record customer questions and objections.\n- Stop if the loss limit or a critical risk is reached.\n- Avoid changing several variables simultaneously.\nWeek 2: Improve the biggest bottleneck\n- If clicks are weak, improve the hook and audience relevance.\n- If page engagement is weak, improve clarity and product demonstration.\n- If carts do not become purchases, review price, shipping, checkout, and trust.\n- If orders are unprofitable, revisit the offer and cost structure.\n- Contact customers when appropriate to understand issues.\n- Check supplier processing and delivery performance.\nWeek 3: Build creative depth\n- Identify the strongest customer insight.\n- Create new original variations around that insight.\n- Test a different audience or use case only when there is a clear hypothesis.\n- Improve FAQs and objection handling.\n- Review refund reasons and product feedback.\n- Stop promoting any claim that cannot be substantiated.\nWeek 4: Decide whether the business deserves more capital\nReview:\n- Total sales and net contribution\n- Advertising spend and acquisition cost\n- Refund and chargeback rate\n- Supplier reliability\n- Delivery performance\n- Customer satisfaction\n- Repeat purchase potential\n- Cash-flow requirements\n- Compliance and operational workload\nChoose one of three outcomes:\n1. Continue: the product has positive economics, acceptable fulfilment, and a credible path to more demand.\n2. Improve: the product has some evidence but needs a specific fix.\n3. Stop: the product, supplier, claims, or economics do not justify further investment.\nDo not keep a product alive because of the time already invested."
      ]
